# Closure parser
# --------------
# The vendored subset is the include-closure of the seed list under a
# parser that handles BOTH `#include "..."` (quoted) AND `#include
# <...>` (angle-bracket) syntaxes. CUTLASS / CuTe sources use both
# forms for local dependencies (e.g., `#include <cute/config.hpp>`,
# `#include "cutlass/array.h"`). The closure regression test
# `tests/test_portable_lcd_cutlass_upstream_closure.py` enforces both
# forms; only `cutlass/<...>` and `cute/<...>` paths are treated as
# local. All other angle-bracket includes are treated as system /
# CUDA toolkit headers and skipped.

# Authoritative regeneration / verification tool
# ----------------------------------------------
# The single source of truth for the closure parser, the seed list,
# and the manifest is the committed tool
# `workloads/portable_lcd/cutlass/tools/recompute_subset.py`.
#
# To verify the on-disk subset is exactly the closure of the seed
# list under a fresh upstream snapshot:
#
#   python3 workloads/portable_lcd/cutlass/tools/recompute_subset.py \
#       --source-include-root <CUTLASS-v3.8.0>/include \
#       --dest-upstream workloads/portable_lcd/cutlass/upstream \
#       --mode check
#
# To rebuild the subset after a tag bump:
#
#   python3 workloads/portable_lcd/cutlass/tools/recompute_subset.py \
#       --source-include-root <CUTLASS-vX.Y>/include \
#       --dest-upstream workloads/portable_lcd/cutlass/upstream \
#       --mode sync
#
# A self-consistency variant (use the vendored tree itself as the
# source) catches closure leaks without a fresh upstream snapshot
# and is exercised by
# `tests/test_portable_lcd_cutlass_recompute_subset.py`:
#
#   python3 workloads/portable_lcd/cutlass/tools/recompute_subset.py \
#       --source-include-root workloads/portable_lcd/cutlass/upstream \
#       --dest-upstream workloads/portable_lcd/cutlass/upstream \
#       --mode check

# Arch-specialized headers in the closure
# ---------------------------------------
# The vendored subset is the include-closure under the documented
# parser, NOT a hand-curated minimality claim. CUTLASS / CuTe
# umbrella headers (notably `cute/atom/mma_atom.hpp` and
# `cute/atom/copy_atom.hpp`) include their architecture-specialized
# trait siblings unconditionally:
#
#   #include <cute/atom/mma_traits_sm80.hpp>
#   #include <cute/atom/mma_traits_sm90.hpp>
#   #include <cute/atom/mma_traits_sm90_gmma.hpp>
#   #include <cute/atom/mma_traits_sm100.hpp>
#
# Consequently the vendored closure contains headers such as:
#
#   cute/arch/mma_sm70.hpp                cute/arch/mma_sm75.hpp
#   cute/arch/mma_sm80.hpp                cute/arch/mma_sm90.hpp
#   cute/arch/mma_sm90_gmma.hpp           cute/arch/mma_sm90_gmma_ext.hpp
#   cute/arch/mma_sm90_gmma_sparse.hpp    cute/arch/mma_sm90_gmma_sparse_ext.hpp
#   cute/arch/mma_sm100.hpp               cute/arch/mma_sm100_umma.hpp
#   cute/arch/copy_sm80.hpp               cute/arch/copy_sm90.hpp
#   cute/arch/copy_sm90_tma.hpp           cute/arch/copy_sm100.hpp
#   cute/arch/copy_sm100_tma.hpp          cute/arch/cluster_sm90.hpp
#   cute/atom/copy_traits_sm90.hpp        cute/atom/copy_traits_sm90_tma.hpp
#   cute/atom/copy_traits_sm100.hpp       cute/atom/copy_traits_sm100_tma.hpp
#   cute/atom/mma_traits_sm90.hpp         cute/atom/mma_traits_sm90_gmma.hpp
#   cute/atom/mma_traits_sm100.hpp        cutlass/arch/mma_sm90.h
#
# These files contain GMMA, TMA, sm_100 / UMMA, and other
# Hopper / Blackwell-only material. They are vendored because the
# preprocessor needs them on disk to parse the aggregate CuTe
# headers, NOT because the consuming kernel emits any forbidden
# instruction. The architectural-LCD enforcement happens at three
# orthogonal layers:
#
#   1. Build-flag layer: every consuming kernel in this subtree
#      compiles with compute_80 / sm_86 / sm_90 / sm_120 base-real
#      arch flags only -- never sm_90a / sm_120a / sm_120f. The
#      compute_80 PTX path lowers all paths to Sm80 ISA, so guarded
#      Hopper / Blackwell CuTe code paths are dead-stripped at
#      ptxas time.
#   2. Source-instantiation layer: the consuming kernel only
#      instantiates the Sm80 mainloop / Conv2dFprop entry points
#      (e.g., `cutlass::gemm::collective::CollectiveMma<sm_80, ...>`,
#      `cutlass::conv::kernel::DefaultConv2dFprop<...>`); the
#      Hopper / Blackwell-only template specialisations are never
#      reached.
#   3. SASS-verification layer: the fatbin-slice verifier
#      (`tools/build/verify_lcd_arch_slices.sh`) scans the compiled
#      sm_120 SASS for forbidden mnemonics (`UTC*MMA`, `LDT`,
#      `STT`) and fails the gate if any leak through. The static
#      guard pytest enforces no forbidden CUPTI / NCU / nvidia-smi
#      surfaces are introduced in active source.
#
# Until a future round actually consumes CUTLASS in a compiled
# kernel (i.e., lands `conv2d_implicit_gemm_bf16_sm80`), the
# vendored subset is parse-only and emits no SASS.

